## The Fix We Landed On: Batch Review Gate

This is the single highest-leverage fix — higher priority than more prompt engineering, higher than citations, higher than the policy wording itself.

**Why:** prompt improvements reduce *how often* something goes wrong. They can't catch it *when* it does — no self-check exists in the current pipeline. A "review" step that reads but can't block/change anything is theater, not review, and shouldn't be claimed in the policy.

### The Pipeline (Plain Version)

```
AI generates a batch (50-100 pages)
        ↓
Pages sit in "pending review" — NOT published yet
        ↓
Human samples 10% of the batch against a fixed checklist
        ↓
   PASS all 6 checks → whole batch publishes
   FAIL any check    → NOTHING in the batch publishes;
                        fix the prompt; re-run the batch
        ↓
Log result: batch #, reviewer, date, pass/fail, what failed
```

### The Checklist (6 items, ~2-3 min per sampled page)

1. **Not templated** — no repeated opener style section-to-section, no forced exact-match keyword phrase repeated 3+ times
2. **No overclaimed expertise** — nothing implying "I'm a licensed nail tech" / "I tested this myself" when untrue
3. **Citation present where required** — if the page makes a health/skin/science claim, is there a real authoritative source linked? (Skip this check if no such claim exists on the page.)
4. **Matches actual site positioning** — nothing contradicting the editorial policy page (no fake personal testing, no overstated research claims)
5. **Reads distinct from recent samples** — not the same rhythm/structure/trick as the last few pages checked (cross-page uniformity check)
6. **No obvious factual error** — plain human eyeball check

**Why this makes the policy true:** this is literally what turns "Ri and Jyoti stand behind every piece" from aspirational into demonstrable — and the log is the proof if it's ever questioned.

**Cost reality:** ~20-30 minutes checking 10 pages decides whether 100 pages publish. Not full manual editing — a gate, not a rewrite step.

